      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve for the volume ratio of the given problem, begin by understanding what a cubic inch represents. The edge of the cube is six inches, and each side's length corresponds to a cubic inch, like so:\n\n\\[\n6 \\text{ inches}^{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, compute the volume of the second cube as it has a different edge length. First, convert the inches into feet, then calculate the volume using the formula for the volume of a cube, given as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Volume} = \\text{edge length}^{3}\n\\]\n\nSince there are 12 inches in a foot, one foot is equivalent to 12 inches. Therefore, the edge length of the second cube can be converted into inches:\n\n\\[\n1 \\text{ foot} = 12 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, compute the volume:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Volume} = (12 \\text{ inches})^{3}\n\\]\n\nExpand the exponent to get the volume in cubic inches:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Volume} = 12 \\times 12 \\times 12 = 1728 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nWe now have the value of the voluems for both cubes. To find the ratio, express the volume of the smaller cube with six inches in the numerator:\n\n\\[\n6 \\text{ inches}^{3}\n\\]\n\nAnd express the volume of the larger cube in the denominator:\n\n\\[\n1728 \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, find the division of these two volumes to get the ratio:\n\n\\[\n\\text{volume ratio} = \\frac{6^{3}}{1728}\n\\]\n\nFirst, compute the value of the numerator:\n\n\\[\n6^{3} = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216\n\\]\n\nSo, the ratio simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{216}{1728}\n\\]\n\nSolve this fraction:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{216}{1728} = \\frac{1}{8}\n\\]\n\nSo, the ratio of the volumes is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}\n\\]",
